OHIRA-0005

Plugin SDK & Extension Architecture

Project: Ohira

Document ID: OHIRA-0005

Version: 1.0

Status: Founder's Draft


---

Purpose

The Plugin SDK defines how external developers can extend Ohira without modifying the core platform.

The SDK provides a stable contract for:

Framework plugins

Database plugins

Cloud plugins

Authentication plugins

Infrastructure plugins

AI integrations

Report generators

Custom analyzers


The goal is to make Ohira extensible by design.


---

Design Principles

1. Core Never Changes

New technologies should be added by installing plugins—not by changing the Ohira Core.

Examples:

@ohira/plugin-next
@ohira/plugin-prisma
@ohira/plugin-react
@ohira/plugin-go
@ohira/plugin-django


---

2. One Plugin, One Responsibility

Each plugin should focus on a single technology.

For example:

Next.js plugin → Next.js only

Prisma plugin → Prisma only

Docker plugin → Docker only


Avoid combining unrelated technologies into a single plugin.


---

3. Safe Execution

Plugins run in isolation.

If one plugin fails:

Log the error.

Mark the plugin as failed.

Continue analysis with the remaining plugins.


A faulty plugin must never stop the entire analysis process.


---

Plugin Lifecycle

Install
   │
Discover
   │
Validate
   │
Initialize
   │
Analyze
   │
Generate Knowledge
   │
Report Results
   │
Unload

Each stage has a defined interface and lifecycle hook.


---

Plugin Manifest

Every plugin includes a manifest describing its metadata.

Example:

{
  "id": "@ohira/plugin-prisma",
  "name": "Prisma Plugin",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Ohira Team",
  "description": "Analyzes Prisma schemas and migrations.",
  "requires": {
    "ohira": "^1.0.0"
  }
}


---

Standard Plugin Interface

Every plugin implements the same contract:

export interface OhiraPlugin {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  version: string;

  supports(project: Project): Promise<boolean>;

  initialize(context: PluginContext): Promise<void>;

  analyze(context: AnalysisContext): Promise<KnowledgeNode[]>;

  dispose(): Promise<void>;
}

This guarantees that plugins can be loaded consistently by the Core Engine.


---

Plugin Context

Each plugin receives a context object containing:

Project root

Configuration

Logger

File system utilities

Cache manager

Event bus

Shared services


Plugins should never access global state directly.


---

Events

Plugins can subscribe to lifecycle events.

Examples:

project.loaded

analysis.started

analysis.completed

report.generated

plugin.loaded

plugin.failed

Future versions may expose a richer event system.


---

Collectors

A collector extracts structured information from a specific technology.

Example:

export interface Collector {
    collect(): Promise<KnowledgeNode[]>;
}

Collectors should only collect data—they should not perform reporting or business logic.


---

Analyzers

Analyzers transform collected data into meaningful insights.

For example:

Collector:

Found 18 API routes.

Analyzer:

All admin routes require authentication.

Collectors gather facts.

Analyzers derive insights.


---

Mappers

Mappers convert raw technology-specific data into Ohira's common knowledge model.

Example:

Prisma model:

model User

becomes:

{
  "type": "Entity",
  "name": "User"
}

This allows every plugin to contribute to a shared vocabulary.


---

Permissions

Plugins should explicitly declare the resources they require.

Examples:

{
  "permissions": [
    "filesystem.read",
    "git.read",
    "docker.inspect"
  ]
}

Future versions may allow users to approve or deny permissions.


---

Dependency Rules

Plugins may depend on:

SDK

Shared utilities


Plugins must not depend on:

Other plugins

Internal Core implementation

Dashboard

CLI


This keeps the plugin ecosystem loosely coupled.


---

Plugin Discovery

Ohira discovers plugins from:

node_modules/

Local plugins/

Workspace plugins/

In the future:

Official Marketplace

Enterprise registries



